**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapter 1 in full; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), What is Historical Geology? in full; [Geodiversidade do Brasil — Cassio Roberto da Silva and contributors](../sources/Geodiversidade-do-Brasil.lingua#r_2ZQN8TT3KZYS9MCFGZV5A1V682), the introduction and opening Earth-history chapters.

Geology connects present observations with Earth materials, processes, and histories. Begin with a question: what explains a layered outcrop, a ridge, a stream deposit, or the ground beneath a proposed structure? A useful answer identifies the observations, competing explanations, constraints, and evidence that could change the interpretation.

Separate what is seen from what is inferred. Grain size, color, an orientation, a contact, and a fossil are observations under a method and scale; depositional environment, deformation history, or formation age are interpretations needing support. A feature can preserve several events, and missing exposure can leave more than one history consistent with the available evidence.

Modern processes help develop explanations of past ones, but rates, conditions, organisms, atmosphere, and tectonic settings have changed. Geological reasoning combines physical/chemical laws, historical ordering, comparison, measurement, and tests of alternative accounts. A plausible story becomes stronger through specific independent constraints.

**Work:** describe one accessible example or authorized published outcrop image. Write an observation-only account, two possible explanations, and an observation that would distinguish them. Identify what cannot be inferred from a single view. Keep the original record so a later reinterpretation does not erase its evidence.

Read the complete opening chapters and the Brazilian overview alongside the English programme. [Philosophy](../../../../philosophy/Philosophy.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001001) and [Inquiry — shared ways to understand and investigate](../../../../inquiry/Inquiry.lingua#r_75MNKZQKNB0XBG021SWWW2HASB) provide a return for questions about evidence, models, and historical explanation. The later field and scholarly work deepens this same beginning rather than replacing it with memorized rock names.

**Continue:** [Keep observations and interpretations connected but distinguishable](Observation-description-and-interpretation.lingua#r_5K44604V7MR09287Y20AWVNRXB).

